# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, NVFP4 (RadixArk's modelopt build: ~176B total with the 51B n-gram table, 6B
# active, 512 experts, vision). TESTED: these exact bytes drove two dresser draws on 2026-09-12 and both
# produced a coherent object - spark/results/dresser-flash-next-medium/ is one of them.
#
# Stock vLLM cannot fit it on one 121 GB Spark (135 GB of weights), so it is served by
# blazux/qwen3.8-Flash-DGX at bd60fcb1b492ca920f74df7462f05da7b6d98f73: the official
# vllm/vllm-openai:qwen38-flash-next@sha256:fc120ece0a388cc0aa1caad4a9f1cd92113484ab7ec2fd0efadd62585be05bf8
# plus 10 patches, built locally as qwen38-flash-dgx:bd60fcb, which mmaps the n-gram table from NVMe so
# ~76-78 GiB sit on the card at 0.80. `spark/serve.sh qwen3.8-flash-next-nvfp4` does all of that for you
# from the SERVE_EXTERNAL_* fields below: clone at the pin, build if the image is absent, start it with
# these settings, and drop the --restart unless-stopped their script hardcodes. It names the container
# ARTICRAFT_SERVE_NAME (default articraft-serve), like every other server this repo starts, so the next
# serve replaces it instead of colliding with it on the port. The runs recorded in spark/results/ were
# taken on a container started by hand as qwen38-flash; that name is theirs and is not edited.
#
# TIGHT ON UNIFIED MEMORY WHILE THIS IS SERVING - budget for it. On a 121 GiB Spark this container
# alone left `free -h` reading ~106 GiB used / ~12 GiB free (2026-09-15, container up ~23h, idle).
# That is normal steady state, not a leak - but it means very little headroom for anything else on
# the box: a host-level low-memory guard (this one on a Claude Code background task; the same class
# of thing could be a systemd OOM policy or the kernel OOM killer elsewhere) can kill an unrelated
# background process - including the `articraft generate` client itself if it's backgrounded - while
# this server just sits there fine. If a generation or build vanishes with no error in its own log,
# check `free -h` and whatever killed it before assuming the client or Articraft is at fault; running
# `articraft generate` from a foreground/detached process rather than a memory-managed background
# job avoids it being a target.
# Licence: qwen-community-1.0 (the checkpoint carries it; review it before any use beyond local).
MODEL_STATUS=tested

# The model the server loads, pinned. Their scripts/serve.sh resolves the cache's refs/main; serve.sh
# checks afterwards that the container really got this revision.
SERVE_MODEL=RadixArk/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-NVFP4
SERVE_REVISION=7b719225242aacd3dbd3f9407468c2ee9a9d2594

# --- the external server, pinned -----------------------------------------------------------------
# These are the exact bytes the recorded runs used, and spark/serve.sh drives them: it clones
# SERVE_EXTERNAL_REPO at SERVE_EXTERNAL_COMMIT, builds SERVE_EXTERNAL_TAG from it if that image is not
# on the box (which pulls SERVE_EXTERNAL_BASE_IMAGE, ~20 GB, once), then runs their scripts/serve.sh.
# That project is driven, never patched and never vendored: it is Apache-2.0 and it stays a clone at
# this commit. Its checkout lives at SERVE_EXTERNAL_DIR (default ~/src/<repo name>).
SERVE_EXTERNAL_REPO=https://github.com/blazux/qwen3.8-Flash-DGX
SERVE_EXTERNAL_COMMIT=bd60fcb1b492ca920f74df7462f05da7b6d98f73
SERVE_EXTERNAL_BASE_IMAGE=vllm/vllm-openai:qwen38-flash-next@sha256:fc120ece0a388cc0aa1caad4a9f1cd92113484ab7ec2fd0efadd62585be05bf8
SERVE_EXTERNAL_TAG=qwen38-flash-dgx:bd60fcb
# Speculative tokens from the model's MTP head, passed to their script as MTP. THEIR DEFAULT IS 2 AND
# THE RECORDED RUNS HAD IT OFF: the container that produced spark/results/dresser-flash-next-medium/
# carries no --speculative-config (checked with `docker inspect`, 2026-09-15). Changing this makes the
# published numbers stop describing what the script produces.
SERVE_EXTERNAL_MTP=0
# The model name the server answers to - blazux's serve.sh hardcodes it and demo_dresser.sh sends it.
# This is NOT the container name (see above): the container is ARTICRAFT_SERVE_NAME.
SERVE_NAME=qwen3.8-flash-next
# The capacity flags serve.sh hands their script (CTX, GPU_MEM, SEQS), read from the running container
# that produced the published result rather than from their defaults (SEQS=8 there).
SERVE_MAX_MODEL_LEN=262144
SERVE_GPU_UTIL=0.80
SERVE_MAX_SEQS=4
# Passed through their EXTRA, which they expand unquoted: serve.sh strips the spaces out of this JSON
# so it stays one argv word. Keep it above ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_MAX_IMAGES.
SERVE_LIMIT_MM='{"image":8}'
# Unused here (spark/serve.sh reads them only for the models it serves itself; blazux's serve.sh sets
# its own): SERVE_IMAGE, SERVE_TOOL_PARSER (qwen3_coder there), SERVE_REASONING_PARSER (qwen3),
# SERVE_SPECULATIVE (SERVE_EXTERNAL_MTP above is what reaches the server), SERVE_EXTRA_ARGS.

# --- client: what Articraft sends ----------------------------------------------------------------
ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_CONTEXT_WINDOW_TOKENS=262144
ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_IMAGES=1
ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_MAX_IMAGES=6
ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=32768
# Its chat template and tokenizer are byte-identical to Qwen3.8-27B's, whose template was measured: by
# default it replays every earlier turn's reasoning into every request (+50,249 tokens at the last turn
# of a recorded 67-turn run); off, it renders within 38 tokens of Qwen3.6's template.
# reasoning_effort: the template's default is xhigh. At low this model ran 98 turns without once calling
#   compile and produced nothing; at medium the same prompt on the same server compiled at turns 63/71/76
#   and ended in final_response with a coherent dresser (6,078 s, 90,510 output tokens).
ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_CHAT_TEMPLATE_KWARGS='{"preserve_thinking": false, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}'
# A known cost of that template: the budget's forced close sends enable_thinking=false, which drops the
# reasoning-effort sentence from the system message, so each forced close prefills the context again.
ARTICRAFT_OPENROUTER_THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS=8192
# A sliver triangle is a tessellation artifact -- in the dresser runs, a 9 um strip along a drawer front,
# invisible in any render -- and on its own it blocked five compiles on this bench, twice at the very end
# of a run that had nothing else wrong (nogate d3 turn 141, xhigh-1 d3 turn 96). With this on, a sliver
# finding is reported as a warning the model still reads instead of failing the compile, but ONLY when
# nothing else in that compile's report is wrong: any other failure, any diagnostic (overlap, a loose
# part, disconnected geometry) or a second mesh issue keeps the block. Default off, so every other model
# file and every hosted provider is unchanged.
ARTICRAFT_MESH_SLIVERS_NONBLOCKING_IF_ALONE=true
